// LobeHub — a magpie agent plugin that only names and draws LobeHub.
//
// magpie loads this module in moejs, in its own process: no Node APIs and
// no imports outside the plugin folder. It exports one `agent` object,
// which magpie checks as internal/agentplug's Desc.
//
// What the id is for: loading the module makes `lobehub` a known agent
// (usage.PluginAgents, from agentplug.List), so usage.AgentOf() can name a
// request by it, the Usage and Sessions pages draw that name with this
// row's name and icon (usageapi.Clients is agent.Clients, "every agent,
// detected or not"), and a ClientAgents pair may name it as the agent a
// client is counted as (countClient asks usage.IsAgent).
//
// What it does not do: put a row on the Agents page. That page lists
// agent.Detected(), which wants a config file that exists, a folder, a bin
// on PATH or a sign-in. This plugin has deliberately none of those, so
// there is no row there, nothing to connect and nothing to configure.
//
// `config` + `model` are the inert placeholder the schema needs (agentplug:
// a module with neither a model key nor a connect has nothing to offer),
// and the path is this plugin's own, outside every LobeHub config
// directory. Nothing writes it: no row is listed to pick a model on.
// Creating that file by hand would make magpie detect the agent and list
// it — which buys nothing; the name and the icon do not need it.
//
// `ua` is what names LobeHub's requests: magpie's usage.AgentOf() takes
// the User-Agent's product token, lower-cases it, and matches this list by
// prefix, so "lobehub/2.2.19" and "lobehub" both count and "lobechat/1.0"
// does not.
//
// Nothing else goes in this list. AgentOf() is not a display rule: magpie
// reads it for the request's identity — the agent named on the Routing
// page, the caller keys, the model catalogue an agent is shown, its caps
// and budgets — so teaching it a deployment's own product token would move
// that traffic under this name everywhere, not only on the Usage and
// Sessions pages. A one-letter token is worse still: the match is by
// prefix, so "k" swallows every other "k..." client.
//
// A deployment whose gateway sends its own token is mapped instead, and
// only where it is reported: settings.ClientAgents (single client counted
// as an agent magpie knows) read back by usage.ReportedAs. scripts/
// arrange-client.mjs sets that pair through magpie's own API, and magpie's
// gateway keeps the client's own name for everything it decides.
//
// `icon` is a data:image URI on purpose: magpie decodes it once, keeps it
// in its own icons folder and serves it from there, so the row needs no
// network. It embeds the official LobeHub Color Logo from lobe-icons:
// https://raw.githubusercontent.com/lobehub/lobe-icons/refs/heads/master/packages/static-png/light/lobehub-color.png
// Keep it byte-for-byte in sync with assets/lobehub-color.png; selfcheck
// verifies both the embedded PNG and its packaged source asset.

export const agent = {
  id: 'lobehub',
  name: 'LobeHub',
  config: '~/.config/magpie-agent-lobehub/placeholder.json',
  format: 'json',
  model: 'model',
  ua: ['lobehub'],
  icon: 'data:image/png;base64,',
};
